{
  "rationale": "The binding constraint is accuracy, not speed: with N micro-rotations the residual angle error is ~2^-(N-1) and the output LSB is 2^-(W-2), so max_abs_err <= 2^-10 needs roughly W >= 12 and N >= 11. The throughput floor of 1 MSPS is trivial for every family (even iterative at N=14 with Fmax ~100 MHz gives several MSPS), so throughput does not shape the front. The objectives therefore reduce to a pure area-vs-accuracy trade-off, and the HV reference point (1500, 10) means only designs with accuracy_bits >= 10 and luts_plus_ffs <= 1500 contribute. Round 1 should (a) put most budget on the family that shares its datapath and should give the smallest area (iterative), (b) probe pipelined_m, the only real area competitor because for small N it trades barrel shifters for a few register stages, and (c) cheaply confirm that unrolled_k and pipelined are dominated. Ranges are kept wide enough to bracket the accuracy threshold (W 11..20, N 10..20) and the guard-bit axes, so later rounds can narrow around the feasible corner and the upper end of the front.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Shared datapath (3 adders + 2 barrel shifters + FSM) with only ~3W state is the natural minimum-area candidate, and at N~12-16 it still clears 1 MSPS by a wide margin. This family should own the low-area end of the front and the selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs), so it gets the largest share and full guard-bit/rounding coverage to find the smallest W,N that reaches 10 bits.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 11, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "family": "iterative",
      "budget_share": 0.55
    },
    {
      "why": "Closest area competitor to iterative: fixed (wiring-only) shifts instead of barrel shifters, paid for with ceil(N/m) register stages. For small N and large m this can approach iterative's area, and it is the only other family that could plausibly sit on the area-vs-accuracy front, so it deserves a real probe across the full m range.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 11, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "budget_share": 0.20
    },
    {
      "why": "Replicates the datapath k times, so for k>=2 it should cost more area than iterative at equal accuracy and is expected to be dominated. A small share is enough to confirm this and to catch any surprise where a k=2 schedule with fewer guard bits lands on the front.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 11, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "budget_share": 0.15
    },
    {
      "why": "One full register stage per micro-rotation is the most area-expensive structure for N>=11 and its 1 result/cycle throughput is far beyond what the 1 MSPS constraint needs, so it is almost certainly dominated. A minimal share is kept only as a sanity check on the cost model and to bound the front from above.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 11, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "family": "pipelined",
      "budget_share": 0.10
    }
  ]
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The feasible front (27 designs, all from the `iterative` family) already spans the accuracy objective well: 10.6..23.7 bits against the HV ref of 10. But it occupies only a narrow area band, luts_plus_ffs 266..667 against the HV reference of 1500 — i.e. the front dies out well before the reference point, and no design between ~667 and 1500 area is on it. Combined with the per-family evidence that pipelined (best feasible area 1810) and pipelined_m (best feasible area 924) are strictly dominated for a spec that only needs >=1 MSPS (iterative already yields 11 MSPS at the frontier, throughput is not an objective), the entire trade-off curve lives in `iterative`, so mapping should be done over that family's full registry ranges. Two deficits remain that a full-range NSGA-II pass can close: (a) the low-area endpoint — min data_width on the front is 16 (10.6 bits), while `iterative` was feasible down to data_width 14, so a slightly cheaper design that still clears the 2^-10 constraint may exist below 266; (b) the high-accuracy endpoint — max seen is 23.69 bits at data_width 27/n_iter 26, with data_width 28 and n_iter up to 30 only partially probed, so higher-accuracy (higher-area) points that raise HV toward the 1500 ref are unexplored. Round 3 is therefore best spent letting code map the whole front over the full `iterative` ranges seeded with the current front; the selection rule's pick (min luts_plus_ffs = 266) is already on the front, so this round is purely about widening HV coverage before the final round."
}
